<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-list">
<details>
<summary>Can I transcribe a YouTube video directly in Chrome?</summary>
<p>Yes. Audicap can capture audio from a regular YouTube Chrome tab and turn it into live transcript text.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Does Audicap download the YouTube video?</summary>
<p>No. Audicap strictly captures the audio playing in your active Chrome tab. It does not download video files, making the process faster and more respectful of copyright boundaries.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Does it work with unlisted or private YouTube videos?</summary>
<p>Yes. Since Audicap transcribes the audio directly from your browser tab, it works on any video you have access to play on your screen, including unlisted, private, or age-restricted content.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>How does background noise affect transcription?</summary>
<p>High-quality, clear speech yields the best results. Videos with heavy background music, multiple people talking over each other, strong wind noise, or generally poor audio quality may result in lower accuracy or missed words.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Is there a noticeable delay during live transcription?</summary>
<p>The transcription is nearly real-time. Text typically appears within a second of the spoken words, making it highly suitable for live reading and following along with the video.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Can I export YouTube transcripts as subtitles?</summary>
<p>Yes. Audicap supports export formats including SRT, which contains timestamps. You can use this file as a subtitle draft in any standard video editing software.</p>
</details>
</div>
<h2>Limitations and Things to Keep in Mind</h2>
<p>While Chrome-based transcription is powerful, it has limitations. It requires an active internet connection to process the audio through AI models. Additionally, if your computer lags or the Chrome tab crashes, the transcription might pause. Always ensure you export your text after a long session to prevent accidental data loss. Lastly, remember that no AI is 100% perfect—always review critical transcripts for accuracy, especially when dealing with highly technical terminology or names.</p>
